10^4$, $10^8$ and $10^{16}$, and $(M_r(N)-\frac Nr(w-2+\frac1w))/(N/(2L))-1$ falls to $3.5\cdot10^{-15}$ at $L=10^{16}$. Solving $M_r(N_0)=Y$ through the parametric form, the omitted inverse constant is recovered as $-1.272589$ ($r=1$) and $-2.158883$ ($r=2$) at $v=10^{15}$, and the ratio of Part~II's shortfall to $r|1+\frac r2-2\log(4r)|Y/v^3$ is $1.000000$ there ($r=1$). It also confirmed $w-t\to\frac r2$ ($0.999999$ at $L=10^6$ for $r=2$). This was a careful reading with numerical tests, not a formal verification. (Dated note, 5~October 2026.) A second independent check, of Proposition~\ref{lam:bg:prop:tilt} alone and with its own programs, found \eqref{lam:bg:eq:ratio-tilt}, the constants $\kappa_K$ and consequence~(1) valid, and consequence~(2) valid for large $N$, with the two wording fixes made in its statement (dated note after the proof). It tested \eqref{lam:bg:eq:ratio-tilt} at $b\ge\lceil\mu-Ku\rceil$ for $K=0,\frac12,1,2$: in exact integers for $u\le12$ ($b\le60$; the totals recover A135501 and A220894), on the full ratio $R(b,u)$ in scaled floating point to $u=140$, and on the single-spine ratio, which is at most $R(b,u)$, to $u=3000$, with no failure and large slack (at $u=3000$: $202$, $562$, $1498$ and $11\,086$ for $K=0,\frac12,1,2$). Over the window of consequence~(2) the margin $\alpha-\frac12(H_u-1)$ is at least $0.71$ for $r=1$ (at $N=10$) and grows like $\frac12\log u_*$; for $r=2$ its least value, always on the upper side of the window and for large $N$ at its edge $u\approx u_*(1+1/\log N)$, is $-0.234$ at $N=20$, $-0.043$ at $N=100$, $-0.058$ at $N=10^6$ and $-0.063$ at $N=10^{12}$, with limit $\log2-\frac12-\frac\gamma2\approx-0.0955$ there. So for $r=2$ the hypothesis of Remark~\ref{lam:bg:rem:ratio}(2) fails on part of the window for all large $N$, and Proposition~\ref{lam:bg:prop:tilt} is needed there.
 
